The `handleSessionInput` function sets the session to `null` when the input is empty or only whitespace. However, when a user selects "Load existing session..." from the dropdown (which has an empty value), this will clear the session and also clear the message history due to the `clearMessages()` call in the `setSession` function. This creates unexpected behavior where selecting the placeholder option clears the current session.
```suggestion

    // If the placeholder option (empty value) is selected, do not clear the session.
    // Instead, keep the current session and restore the input field to it.
    if (!value) {
      sessionInput = $currentSession || '';
      return;
    }

    sessionInput = value;
    setSession(value);
```

When a session is selected from the dropdown, the session name is set but the actual session messages are not loaded into the chat. The component should load the session's message history when a session is selected from the dropdown, otherwise users won't see the previous conversation context.
```suggestion
    setSession(value || null);
    // Load the selected session's message history so the chat reflects prior context
    if (value) {
      sessionAPI.loadSessionMessages(value);
    }
```

This is called without error handling. Compare to the similar code in `SessionManager` (lines 15-21):

```javascript
onMount(async () => {
    try {
      await sessionAPI.loadSessions();
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Failed to load sessions:', error);
    }
  });
```

So it should be something similar, like:

```javascript
onMount(async () => {
  fetchStrategies();
  try {
    await sessionAPI.loadSessions();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to load sessions:', error);
  }
  sessionInput = $currentSession || '';
});
```

In addition, the session handling being in two places (here and in `SessionManager`) can introduce potential race conditions and unnecessary API calls. The session loading should happen once, ideally when the application initializes.
